title: 'Fixes for issues reported by the Runtime Whitebox Fuzzer'
doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    The leaf branch of `is_scheduling_parent_valid` computed the leaf's successor slot with plain
    `u64` addition on a slot taken from a BABE pre-digest in a peer-supplied header. A leaf slot of
    `u64::MAX` overflowed unconditionally: debug builds panicked, and release builds wrapped the sum
    to `0`, which matched a `current_slot` of `0` and accepted a provably invalid scheduling parent.

    The increment is now checked, so a leaf slot with no successor is rejected instead of
    overflowing. Reported by the Runtime Whitebox Fuzzer.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `pallet-elections-phragmen::submit_candidacy` allowed one candidate more than the documented
    `MaxCandidates` limit: the guard compared the current candidate count with `<=` instead of `<`,
    so a call made while the list already held `MaxCandidates` entries succeeded and grew it to
    `MaxCandidates + 1`. The guard now rejects such a call with `TooManyCandidates`.

    The `submit_candidacy` benchmark's `c` component range shifts accordingly, to
    `[0, MaxCandidates - 1]`: pre-filling the candidate list up to the limit is no longer a state
    in which the measured call is accepted. Runtimes regenerating weights will see the new range.
    Reported by the Runtime Whitebox Fuzzer.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `pallet-conviction-voting`'s `Tally::add` and `Tally::remove` mutated `support` before checking
    the `ayes`/`nays` arithmetic, so a call that returned `None` on over/underflow left the tally
    partially modified. Both now compute all fields first and only commit once every checked
    operation succeeded. In-tree callers already abort the extrinsic on `None`, so no chain state
    was affected; this hardens the public API for runtimes that call it directly. Reported by the
    Runtime Whitebox Fuzzer.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `cumulus_primitives_timestamp::InherentDataProvider::provide_inherent_data` computed the next
    relay chain slot with plain `u64` arithmetic and truncated the slot duration with `as u64`, so
    an out-of-range slot or slot duration panicked in debug builds and wrapped to an arbitrary
    timestamp in release builds. The arithmetic now saturates. Note that a saturated `u64::MAX`
    timestamp still overflows the unchecked addition in `pallet_timestamp::check_inherent`, a
    pre-existing defect in `sp-timestamp`'s `Add` impl. The constructor has no in-tree callers,
    so no node or chain was affected; this hardens the public API. Reported by the Runtime
    Whitebox Fuzzer.
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    The chain selection subsystem's default `PruneOnly` stagnant check computed its prune cutoff as
    `now - STAGNANT_PRUNE_DELAY` with plain `u64` subtraction. A system wall clock reading below the
    25 hour delay -- an unset RTC, say -- panicked in debug builds. Release builds wrapped the
    cutoff to near `u64::MAX`, dropping stagnant bookkeeping right away instead of respecting the
    delay. The subtraction now saturates. Reported by the Runtime Whitebox Fuzzer.
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    `sc_consensus_slots::proposing_remaining_duration` only clamped its result against
    `max_block_proposal_slot_portion` on the path where slot lenience was applied. With a
    `block_proposal_slot_portion` larger than the maximum, every other path -- no skipped slots,
    unknown parent slot, genesis parent -- could return a proposing duration above the documented
    ceiling. The clamp now applies to all paths. All in-tree node configurations leave
    `max_block_proposal_slot_portion` unset, so no node was affected; this fixes the documented
    contract for the public API. Reported by the Runtime Whitebox Fuzzer.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `pallet-delegated-staking` stored a zero amount `Delegation` when a new delegation was created
    with an amount of zero: `Delegation::update` only dropped zero amount delegations that already
    existed. The delegator was left with a useless storage entry and an extra provider reference,
    which kept the account from being reaped and permanently blocked it from registering as an
    agent. A zero amount delegation is now never stored, on any path that creates one, matching
    what the method already documented, and `delegate_to_agent` returns early for a zero amount
    instead of bonding it. Accounts that already leaked such an entry are not healed by this
    change: they stay stuck, and `pallet_nomination_pools::migrate_delegation` keeps rejecting
    them. Reported by the Runtime Whitebox Fuzzer.
- audience: [Runtime Dev, Runtime User]
  description: |-
    `pallet_scheduler::set_retry` did not enforce its documented contract: it accepted named tasks,
    even though the doc comment states it cannot be used for them and `Error::Named` exists for
    exactly this case. A named task configured this way emitted `RetrySet` with `id: None`, hiding
    the task's name from anyone following the events. `set_retry` now rejects named tasks with
    `Error::Named`, mirroring `do_reschedule`; use `set_retry_named` for those. A `set_retry` call
    that used to succeed on a named task now fails, so callers must switch. No storage migration is
    needed: `Retries` is keyed on the task address alone, so existing entries -- for named tasks
    included -- keep working, and `cancel_retry` can still remove them. One corner loses its last
    escape hatch: a named task whose dispatch was skipped inside `service_task` -- missing
    preimage, or the call itself overweight -- has already lost its `Lookup` entry while staying
    named in the agenda, so `set_retry_named` returns `NotFound` for it and `set_retry` now returns
    `Named`. With a missing preimage the task is dead anyway; an overweight call is only postponed
    and will still run, so that one is left with no way to get a retry configuration. `cancel_named`
    and `reschedule_named` are already unreachable in the same window: the root cause is that
    `service_task` drops the `Lookup` entry before it knows the task completed. The `set_retry`
    benchmark had to stop targeting a named task; weights were not regenerated, since the new setup
    can only measure less. Reported by the Runtime Whitebox Fuzzer.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `pallet-nft-fractionalization::unify` only called `start_destroy` on the fractions asset, so the
    asset was left in `Destroying` state: its `Asset` entry and metadata stayed in storage and the
    metadata deposit stayed reserved, until someone happened to finish the destruction by hand
    (`finish_destroy` is permissionless). `unify` now finishes the destruction itself, which clears
    the metadata and releases the deposit back to the pallet's account -- the account that reserved
    it. With an `AssetIdAllocator` that permits it, the asset ID also becomes reusable; on the Asset
    Hubs (`AutoIncAssetId`) IDs are never reused, so there the gain is the storage and deposit
    cleanup only.

    Finishing is best effort: it cannot succeed while the asset still has asset accounts -- a
    `touch`ed account survives a zero balance -- or approvals, no matter whose those are. In that
    case the asset stays in `Destroying` state as before, and anyone can complete the destruction
    with `destroy_accounts`/`destroy_approvals` + `finish_destroy`. Failing the `unify` instead
    would let anyone lock an NFT for good, since anyone can create such an account or approval.

    Two known gaps are left alone: the metadata deposit is still not refunded to the account that
    paid it during `fractionalize`, it just accumulates in the pallet's account; and `start_destroy`
    can still fail the `unify` outright if the asset has holds or freezes.

    `unify` now does more work than the committed weights account for -- on the success path it
    touches at least `Assets::Metadata`, `Assets::Reserves` and the pallet account's
    `System::Account`, plus whatever the asset registry's destroy callback does -- so every
    committed `pallet_nft_fractionalization` weight file under-declares it, in ref time and in
    proof size. The pallet's own `weights.rs` and the Asset Hub Westend one should be regenerated
    with `/cmd bench` before this merges; the Asset Hub Rococo and staking-async parachain ones are
    not in the benchmark matrix and keep their stale numbers. Reported by the Runtime Whitebox
    Fuzzer.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `pallet-derivatives` never emitted its `DerivativeMappingCreated` event: the
    `DerivativesRegistry::try_register_derivative` implementation, which writes the
    original-to-derivative mapping, deposited `DerivativeCreated` instead. As a result the
    `create_derivative` extrinsic emitted `DerivativeCreated` twice for configurations that store a
    mapping, and the new derivative's local ID -- carried only by `DerivativeMappingCreated` -- was
    never reported. Registration now emits `DerivativeMappingCreated`, and `create_derivative`
    emits `DerivativeCreated` once.

    This changes the events seen by anything registering a mapping outside the extrinsic (for
    example the `RegisterDerivative` XCM creation adapter): it now emits `DerivativeMappingCreated`
    where it used to emit `DerivativeCreated`. No in-tree runtime configures this pallet yet.

    The destroy side is left as it was: `DerivativeDestroyed` is deposited when the mapping is
    dropped, so `destroy_derivative` emits no event at all for a configuration that stores no
    mapping. Fixing that needs a new event and is not done here. Reported by the Runtime Whitebox
    Fuzzer.
crates:
- name: polkadot-collator-protocol
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-elections-phragmen
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-conviction-voting
  bump: patch
- name: cumulus-primitives-timestamp
  bump: patch
- name: polkadot-node-core-chain-selection
  bump: patch
- name: sc-consensus-slots
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-delegated-staking
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-scheduler
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-nft-fractionalization
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-derivatives
  bump: patch
